1
00:00:07,966 --> 00:00:10,301
[upbeat instrumental music plays]

2
00:00:15,515 --> 00:00:16,850
[engine turns over]

3
00:00:18,393 --> 00:00:19,853
[revs]

4
00:00:20,103 --> 00:00:20,937
[horn toots]

5
00:00:24,149 --> 00:00:27,736
[Jerry] This is a 1959 Lambretta Series 2.

6
00:00:27,819 --> 00:00:30,155
Certain machines create obsession.

7
00:00:30,238 --> 00:00:34,325
I am obsessed with Italian scooters
from the '50s and '60s.

8
00:00:34,409 --> 00:00:36,786
And let me tell you why it's special.

9
00:00:38,329 --> 00:00:40,749
When you fall in love
with a vintage machine,

10
00:00:40,832 --> 00:00:43,877
it's not just the machine,
it's the moment in which it lived

11
00:00:43,960 --> 00:00:46,171
and why it was popular at that time.

12
00:00:47,047 --> 00:00:50,717
After World War II, Italy decided
they had to build the cheapest,

13
00:00:50,800 --> 00:00:54,721
most basic, most practical way
to get around. The scooter.

14
00:00:55,013 --> 00:00:59,350
This is the guy who designed one
of the first Vespas, Corradino D'Ascanio.

15
00:00:59,434 --> 00:01:01,770
Don't you wanna be like him? I do.

16
00:01:01,853 --> 00:01:03,188
But here's what happened.

17
00:01:03,271 --> 00:01:04,397
[singing in Italian]

18
00:01:06,316 --> 00:01:09,819
Even though they were designed
to be cheap utilitarian transportation,

19
00:01:09,903 --> 00:01:11,696
it was done by Italians,

20
00:01:11,780 --> 00:01:12,739
so it had beauty,

21
00:01:12,822 --> 00:01:15,366
it had style, it had panache.

22
00:01:15,825 --> 00:01:19,788
Look at the forms, the styling,
the beautiful colors and shapes.

23
00:01:20,080 --> 00:01:22,624
Unlike a motorcycle,
they wanted to cover up the engine,

24
00:01:22,707 --> 00:01:24,584
so there's no gas and oil spatter.

25
00:01:24,667 --> 00:01:27,128
And the front,
so your pants don't get dirty.

26
00:01:27,462 --> 00:01:30,298
Italians, they care about clothes.

27
00:01:30,381 --> 00:01:32,342
Here's one being built in the late '50s.

28
00:01:32,425 --> 00:01:33,760
And here's mine today.

29
00:01:33,843 --> 00:01:35,595
Can you understand why that's fun?

30
00:01:35,678 --> 00:01:36,638
I got the book.

31
00:01:36,721 --> 00:01:37,847
I got the model.

32
00:01:37,931 --> 00:01:39,432
I can't get enough of it.

33
00:01:39,766 --> 00:01:41,392
Scooters were in movies.

34
00:01:41,684 --> 00:01:44,562
Men drove them with sports jackets
and little pocket squares.

35
00:01:44,646 --> 00:01:46,856
Women drove them in dresses
with families.

36
00:01:46,940 --> 00:01:48,900
No helmets, nobody cared.

37
00:01:49,275 --> 00:01:52,362
Remember when life was
kind of a cheap throwaway thing?

38
00:01:52,445 --> 00:01:55,365
Was it more fun? Of course it was!

39
00:01:56,241 --> 00:01:59,369
I always wanted to have
my very special, little Italian machine

40
00:01:59,452 --> 00:02:01,913
with a very special,
little Italian comedian.

41
00:02:01,996 --> 00:02:05,125
The incredibly funny Sebastian Maniscalco.

42
00:02:05,208 --> 00:02:06,417
Amazing!

43
00:02:06,501 --> 00:02:07,710
[audience laughter]

44
00:02:07,794 --> 00:02:08,711
[cell phone beeps]

45
00:02:08,795 --> 00:02:09,712
[Sebastian] Hello?

46
00:02:09,796 --> 00:02:11,172
-[Jerry] Yo.
-[Sebastian] What's up?

47
00:02:11,256 --> 00:02:13,383
[Jerry] Well, welcome back to the show,
Sebastian.

48
00:02:13,466 --> 00:02:16,010
I feel like there's more I'd like to get
out of that head of yours.

49
00:02:16,094 --> 00:02:17,679
[Sebastian] Can't wait to see what it is.

50
00:02:17,762 --> 00:02:20,140
[Jerry] I know the name of the show
is Comedians in Cars,

51
00:02:20,223 --> 00:02:22,767
but today we're gonna do something
a little different.

52
00:02:22,851 --> 00:02:25,061
-[Sebastian] Let's do it.
-[Jerry] This is my scooter.

53
00:02:25,144 --> 00:02:26,396
This is my show.

54
00:02:26,479 --> 00:02:30,066
We're gonna dress sharp and bring
a little two-wheeled elegante

55
00:02:30,150 --> 00:02:32,068
to an Italian neighborhood in my town.

56
00:02:32,360 --> 00:02:34,279
-This is a great moment.
-This is a great moment.

57
00:02:34,362 --> 00:02:36,447
[Jerry] Could this be more perfect? No.

58
00:02:36,990 --> 00:02:38,700
And that's why I'm Jerry Seinfeld,

59
00:02:38,783 --> 00:02:41,661
and this
is Comedians in Cars Getting Coffee.

60
00:02:43,246 --> 00:02:46,499
So, you know I'm fascinated
with, uh, your lifestyle.

61
00:02:46,583 --> 00:02:49,460
First of all, I love the way
you pronounce "fascinated."

62
00:02:49,544 --> 00:02:51,129
-You like that?
-You're not "fascinated."

63
00:02:51,212 --> 00:02:52,463
You're "faescinated."

64
00:02:52,547 --> 00:02:53,464
[both laugh]

65
00:02:59,220 --> 00:03:00,597
So, this is

66
00:03:01,014 --> 00:03:03,141
-how we're gonna get around today.
-This is beautiful.

67
00:03:03,224 --> 00:03:05,602
You know, they used to do six-day races.

68
00:03:05,685 --> 00:03:07,687
-That's what the six is on the front.
-Okay.

69
00:03:07,770 --> 00:03:10,106
And the name of the scooter
is a Sei Giorni.

70
00:03:10,190 --> 00:03:11,524
-Oh, sei giorni.
-Six days.

71
00:03:11,608 --> 00:03:12,775
Sei giorni.

72
00:03:12,859 --> 00:03:14,444
[mid-tempo music plays]

73
00:03:21,201 --> 00:03:23,620
-[Jerry] Wow! This is fun!
-[Sebastian] Oh, yeah.

74
00:03:25,079 --> 00:03:25,997
Doggy!

75
00:03:27,457 --> 00:03:29,375
This really feels like
an Italian scooter fantasy,

76
00:03:29,459 --> 00:03:30,668
-doesn't it?
-Doesn't it?

77
00:03:41,054 --> 00:03:44,015
You are very particular
about every aspect in your life.

78
00:03:44,098 --> 00:03:44,933
Yeah.

79
00:03:45,350 --> 00:03:48,019
There is nothing about which
I am not very particular.

80
00:03:48,102 --> 00:03:49,520
[both laugh]

81
00:03:50,605 --> 00:03:52,649
Or as my mother would say, a pill.

82
00:03:52,732 --> 00:03:54,108
[both laugh]

83
00:03:54,192 --> 00:03:55,318
[upbeat music plays]

84
00:04:02,909 --> 00:04:03,743
[Jerry] Relaxing.

85
00:04:03,826 --> 00:04:05,620
-Oh, relaxing.
-Vacationing.

86
00:04:05,703 --> 00:04:07,830
Enjoying leisure time.

87
00:04:07,914 --> 00:04:09,207
I am not good at that.

88
00:04:09,290 --> 00:04:11,209
Yeah, you told me in the past that

89
00:04:11,292 --> 00:04:13,253
-you have a problem with, uh...
-[Jerry] Yeah.

90
00:04:13,711 --> 00:04:14,545
Depression.

91
00:04:14,629 --> 00:04:17,006
-I get... I have depression.
-[Sebastian laughs]

92
00:04:18,508 --> 00:04:19,717
-This amuses you.
-I...

93
00:04:19,801 --> 00:04:21,219
I... [laughs]

94
00:04:26,391 --> 00:04:30,311
Yeah, I got inspired in Italy
to get my own Vespa, right?

95
00:04:30,395 --> 00:04:33,022
-[Jerry] Oh.
-So, I went there. I saw it, but...

96
00:04:33,272 --> 00:04:34,649
The guys there,

97
00:04:34,732 --> 00:04:37,318
the way they drive
and the way they look...

98
00:04:37,402 --> 00:04:40,071
-Yeah.
-...I tried to replicate that at home.

99
00:04:40,154 --> 00:04:42,490
-Doesn't work.
-[Jerry] Ah, I know.

100
00:04:42,865 --> 00:04:44,951
Jess and I took a trip to Italy, we said,

101
00:04:45,034 --> 00:04:47,495
"Let's have breakfast like this
every morning."

102
00:04:47,578 --> 00:04:50,915
Prosciutto, cheese, salami.

103
00:04:52,208 --> 00:04:53,209
Espresso. 

104
00:04:53,459 --> 00:04:55,336
We tried it twice. It's horrible.

105
00:04:55,420 --> 00:04:57,171
[Sebastian laughs]

106
00:04:57,255 --> 00:04:58,172
Perfect.

107
00:04:59,424 --> 00:05:00,258
Wow.

108
00:05:06,306 --> 00:05:10,310
I love going by myself
to the grocery store.

109
00:05:10,393 --> 00:05:12,061
If I'm going to make a steak,

110
00:05:12,353 --> 00:05:14,397
picking out particular meats.

111
00:05:14,814 --> 00:05:17,066
So, you don't even bother
with the cooking?

112
00:05:17,900 --> 00:05:18,860
-Me?
-Yeah.

113
00:05:19,318 --> 00:05:20,611
-No?
-[Jerry laughs]

114
00:05:28,369 --> 00:05:31,956
A couple Christmases ago,
my wife and I got in a little argument,

115
00:05:32,040 --> 00:05:34,375
and I had a Christmas hat on.

116
00:05:34,459 --> 00:05:35,293
[Jerry laughs]

117
00:05:35,376 --> 00:05:36,502
And, uh...

118
00:05:37,128 --> 00:05:39,297
we started arguing
in front of the family...

119
00:05:39,380 --> 00:05:41,507
-Yeah.
-...which is... was awkward.

120
00:05:41,591 --> 00:05:43,176
-Yeah.
-So, we had to move the argument

121
00:05:43,259 --> 00:05:44,385
into the backyard.

122
00:05:44,469 --> 00:05:47,722
I... It was one of those arguments
where I just took Christmas hat

123
00:05:47,805 --> 00:05:49,682
and I chucked it over the fence.

124
00:05:49,766 --> 00:05:51,559
It landed in the neighbor's yard.

125
00:05:52,060 --> 00:05:54,937
-Did you ever do that?
-Chuck a Christmas hat? No.

126
00:05:55,021 --> 00:05:57,565
So, a little red Santa hat
with the white pompom

127
00:05:57,648 --> 00:05:59,734
-and the white fur border.
-Yeah.

128
00:05:59,817 --> 00:06:02,111
-Right over--
-Wearing that in a...

129
00:06:02,195 --> 00:06:05,948
and being in a middle
of a knockdown drag out,

130
00:06:06,032 --> 00:06:07,742
that's a good comedy scene.

131
00:06:07,825 --> 00:06:08,785
[both laugh]

132
00:06:11,537 --> 00:06:13,206
You got a best friend?

133
00:06:16,084 --> 00:06:18,294
That seems a little juvenile.

134
00:06:18,378 --> 00:06:20,588
[both laugh]

135
00:06:22,590 --> 00:06:25,259
I've told you this before,
but I wanna get it out in the open,

136
00:06:25,343 --> 00:06:26,302
-now that we're...
-Okay.

137
00:06:26,385 --> 00:06:27,637
Your texting...

138
00:06:28,388 --> 00:06:29,263
[Jerry laughs]

139
00:06:29,347 --> 00:06:31,557
is so minimal.

140
00:06:31,641 --> 00:06:33,226
Well, you're not engaging me.

141
00:06:33,434 --> 00:06:34,894
Well, what do you wanna talk about?

142
00:06:34,977 --> 00:06:37,438
First of all, if we wanna talk,
let's talk,

143
00:06:37,522 --> 00:06:39,315
because that's what we do well.

144
00:06:39,399 --> 00:06:40,233
Mm-hmm.

145
00:06:40,316 --> 00:06:42,693
We don't type. We're not typists.

146
00:06:43,486 --> 00:06:44,821
-We're "talkists."
-We are.

147
00:06:44,904 --> 00:06:45,905
-Right?
-[Sebastian laughs]

148
00:06:45,988 --> 00:06:47,365
I don't what the hell you're doing.

149
00:06:47,448 --> 00:06:49,242
You could be in the middle of this
or whatever.

150
00:06:49,325 --> 00:06:51,285
I don't know what your schedule is.
Again...

151
00:06:51,369 --> 00:06:53,830
You don't-- Why would you
have to know anything?

152
00:06:54,288 --> 00:06:56,374
If I'm not free, I won't answer.

153
00:06:56,457 --> 00:06:58,626
You're in an old model.

154
00:06:59,210 --> 00:07:00,169
In the new model...

155
00:07:00,753 --> 00:07:03,506
These people that pick up the phone,
"I can't talk now."

156
00:07:04,340 --> 00:07:05,925
-Why are you answering?
-Yeah.

157
00:07:06,008 --> 00:07:07,343
[Sebastian laughs]

158
00:07:12,473 --> 00:07:16,144
So, how do you do socially
when your wife says,

159
00:07:16,227 --> 00:07:18,980
"Hey, I met this girl and husband, and...

160
00:07:19,397 --> 00:07:20,273
I made a dinner.

161
00:07:20,356 --> 00:07:22,400
We're gonna go out with this couple."

162
00:07:22,483 --> 00:07:24,110
-Does she do that?
-All the time.

163
00:07:24,277 --> 00:07:25,653
-All the... [laughs]
-Yeah.

164
00:07:25,736 --> 00:07:29,574
I don't go near people. I don't chitchat.
All right?

165
00:07:29,657 --> 00:07:30,575
[audience laughter]

166
00:07:30,658 --> 00:07:33,119
I know a lot of people
like to go in the midst of people,

167
00:07:33,202 --> 00:07:35,037
"Oh, hi!"

168
00:07:35,121 --> 00:07:37,123
This is my favorite thing.

169
00:07:37,665 --> 00:07:41,210
Comedians attempting to function
as normal people.

170
00:07:41,294 --> 00:07:42,378
I have problems with it.

171
00:07:42,462 --> 00:07:44,088
[Jerry laughing] Yeah.

172
00:07:44,172 --> 00:07:45,756
I'm not a comfortable guy.

173
00:07:46,090 --> 00:07:48,885
Around a lot of people,
like a dinner party.

174
00:07:48,968 --> 00:07:51,012
-Or a--
-Dinner party, or, I don't know...

175
00:07:51,095 --> 00:07:52,430
-A little event.
-Mm-hmm.

176
00:07:52,513 --> 00:07:53,848
-You go to an event...
-Event.

177
00:07:53,931 --> 00:07:56,517
...and you're kind of, uh,
wandering around.

178
00:07:56,809 --> 00:08:00,021
Like a new puppy
looking for a comfortable spot.

179
00:08:00,104 --> 00:08:03,065
Yes, yes. And I rarely find it.

180
00:08:03,149 --> 00:08:05,818
I'm like a cat. I love to hide.

181
00:08:05,902 --> 00:08:07,361
-[audience laughter]
-All right?

182
00:08:07,737 --> 00:08:11,157
Does your wife understand
this aspect of your personality?

183
00:08:11,782 --> 00:08:14,035
-I feel sorry for her sometimes.
-[Jerry laughs]

184
00:08:14,118 --> 00:08:17,872
Did she know before you got married
that this was what she was signing up for?

185
00:08:17,955 --> 00:08:19,957
She did, but, uh...

186
00:08:20,333 --> 00:08:22,418
I don't know if she knew the extent of it.

187
00:08:22,501 --> 00:08:24,170
Didn't know the extent.

188
00:08:29,342 --> 00:08:31,219
Like, sometimes when I do these things,

189
00:08:31,677 --> 00:08:33,930
I wonder, like, how these guys,

190
00:08:34,555 --> 00:08:36,682
you know, the crew, how they're...

191
00:08:36,766 --> 00:08:39,143
Like, are they, like, going,
"All right already."

192
00:08:39,227 --> 00:08:41,687
Like, I'm in my head sometimes going,

193
00:08:41,771 --> 00:08:44,732
"Man, I hope these guys
are enjoying it."

194
00:08:45,024 --> 00:08:46,192
Do you ever think that?

195
00:08:46,901 --> 00:08:47,735
No.

196
00:08:47,818 --> 00:08:49,612
[Sebastian laughs] 

197
00:08:51,030 --> 00:08:52,240
There's no money in these guys.

198
00:08:52,323 --> 00:08:53,699
[Sebastian laughs]

199
00:08:53,783 --> 00:08:54,825
[Jerry laughs]

200
00:08:54,909 --> 00:08:58,412
There's another audience out there,
that's where the money is.

201
00:08:59,121 --> 00:09:01,666
-But don't you think--
-Inside the lens.

202
00:09:01,749 --> 00:09:03,000
If you look in there,

203
00:09:03,793 --> 00:09:06,128
-that's where the money is.
-[Sebastian laughs]

204
00:09:10,675 --> 00:09:12,552
If you ever left LA, where would you live?

205
00:09:12,635 --> 00:09:15,596
New York City, California, tight,
you know?

206
00:09:15,680 --> 00:09:17,223
-I like it tight.
-You like it tight.

207
00:09:17,306 --> 00:09:19,976
-I'm... Just like my act, I want it tight.
-Tight.

208
00:09:20,059 --> 00:09:22,186
Yeah. I need
a little bit more, like, room.

209
00:09:22,270 --> 00:09:23,104
[Jerry] Okay.

210
00:09:23,688 --> 00:09:26,148
Like, I need... I need, like, grass.

211
00:09:26,524 --> 00:09:28,401
-I love that idea for you.
-You love grass?

212
00:09:28,484 --> 00:09:31,612
I love grass.
And I love to see you in grass.

213
00:09:31,696 --> 00:09:32,780
[Sebastian laughs]

214
00:09:41,414 --> 00:09:43,165
I love the sound of this thing.

215
00:09:43,499 --> 00:09:45,876
I got gasoline in my veins, dude.

216
00:09:45,960 --> 00:09:47,169
[Sebastian laughs]

217
00:09:47,253 --> 00:09:49,672
I just love hearing this thing run.

218
00:10:01,183 --> 00:10:04,478
There's not enough texture there
for a guy like you.

219
00:10:05,563 --> 00:10:07,773
Cultural texture, that's what I miss.

220
00:10:07,857 --> 00:10:10,109
This. These guys. That face.

221
00:10:10,192 --> 00:10:12,778
-Look at that face.  Look at that face.
-[Sebastian laughs]

222
00:10:12,862 --> 00:10:15,364
He looks like they made the sandwich
out of his head.

223
00:10:15,448 --> 00:10:16,657
[Sebastian laughs]

224
00:10:17,199 --> 00:10:19,827
Well, I gotta reevaluate my life now
after this show.

225
00:10:19,910 --> 00:10:21,037
[Jerry laughs]

226
00:10:23,539 --> 00:10:26,917
I have to-- I'm gonna come home
and go, "Babe, there's not enough...

227
00:10:27,418 --> 00:10:29,462
-texture here for us." "What?"
-[Jerry laughs]

228
00:10:31,714 --> 00:10:34,759
What should we get?
We want a big, nice hero.

229
00:10:34,842 --> 00:10:38,471
The number... the number one hero here
is the Italian special.

230
00:10:38,554 --> 00:10:39,847
Stop right there.

231
00:10:39,930 --> 00:10:41,682
["Lambret Twist" in Italian plays]

232
00:10:57,865 --> 00:10:59,408
[Sebastian] This is nice.

233
00:10:59,742 --> 00:11:00,576
[Jerry laughs]

234
00:11:01,077 --> 00:11:01,994
Hmm? This is my fav.

235
00:11:02,078 --> 00:11:02,912
[horn toots]

236
00:11:04,997 --> 00:11:06,123
[bell dings]

237
00:11:12,546 --> 00:11:14,548
So, are you a sandwich guy?

238
00:11:14,632 --> 00:11:16,467
-Do you--
-I am right now, yeah.

239
00:11:20,805 --> 00:11:22,890
-This is a great moment.
-This is a great moment.

240
00:11:27,269 --> 00:11:28,729
Nothing like a good sandwich.

241
00:11:30,314 --> 00:11:31,899
You're gonna have a big family.

242
00:11:32,525 --> 00:11:34,527
-Yeah?
-If I was a younger man,

243
00:11:34,610 --> 00:11:36,654
I would've had six, seven kids.

244
00:11:36,737 --> 00:11:37,697
-Really?
-Yeah.

245
00:11:37,780 --> 00:11:40,157
Did you feel that way going in,
that you wanted--

246
00:11:40,241 --> 00:11:41,075
No.

247
00:11:41,409 --> 00:11:43,452
So, kids made you wanna have more kids?

248
00:11:43,536 --> 00:11:44,370
Sure.

249
00:11:48,874 --> 00:11:49,959
Like a sandwich.

250
00:11:52,086 --> 00:11:54,004
-The more you eat, the more you want.
-Yeah.

251
00:11:55,464 --> 00:11:58,300
There's chaos in my house,
and it's just total madness.

252
00:12:01,011 --> 00:12:01,846
It's the greatest.

253
00:12:03,639 --> 00:12:05,015
What are you doing tonight?

254
00:12:05,975 --> 00:12:07,518
-Nothing.
-Come on over.

255
00:12:09,103 --> 00:12:11,230
-Yeah, that's--
-Can I host you for dinner?

256
00:12:11,480 --> 00:12:13,566
That would... That would be fascinating.

257
00:12:13,649 --> 00:12:15,067
[laughter]

258
00:12:15,151 --> 00:12:17,570
♪ Lambret twist ♪

259
00:12:17,653 --> 00:12:18,654
Scooters!

260
00:12:36,255 --> 00:12:37,673
Do you have pistachio?

261
00:12:37,757 --> 00:12:39,842
[Jerry] I see you have
mint chocolate chip.

262
00:12:39,925 --> 00:12:41,177
That's what I would like.

263
00:12:41,719 --> 00:12:43,721
I'm gonna get the smallest one you got.

264
00:12:44,680 --> 00:12:46,891
I'll have the next one up.

265
00:12:50,269 --> 00:12:51,729
Big cover story today

266
00:12:51,812 --> 00:12:54,106
in the Arts &amp; Leisure of The Times
on Lenny Bruce.

267
00:12:54,190 --> 00:12:55,065
Oh, yeah?

268
00:12:56,984 --> 00:12:58,152
When did he pass away?

269
00:12:58,235 --> 00:12:59,236
Sixty-six.

270
00:12:59,612 --> 00:13:00,654
At the ago of 40.

271
00:13:01,614 --> 00:13:02,448
Of what?

272
00:13:03,324 --> 00:13:04,241
Drug overdose.

273
00:13:06,076 --> 00:13:07,119
Did you ever meet him?

274
00:13:07,203 --> 00:13:08,204
I was eleven.

275
00:13:12,041 --> 00:13:13,959
[Sebastian laughs]

276
00:13:17,880 --> 00:13:20,341
Remember we talked about
that bit about, um,

277
00:13:20,800 --> 00:13:22,468
the basement, the den.

278
00:13:22,551 --> 00:13:24,595
-You bring the girl in the den.
-Yeah.

279
00:13:24,678 --> 00:13:26,388
-Did that ever grow into in anything? 
-Yeah.

280
00:13:26,472 --> 00:13:28,516
-Going to downstairs and--
-Yeah.

281
00:13:29,475 --> 00:13:31,685
-Nothing matches in a basement...
-Yeah, yeah.

282
00:13:31,769 --> 00:13:32,728
...in the '80s.

283
00:13:32,937 --> 00:13:33,854
[Jerry laughs]

284
00:13:34,188 --> 00:13:37,316
That was a piece to the puzzle
of the dancing bit,

285
00:13:37,399 --> 00:13:40,194
because then you'd ask the girl out,
you'd take her out,

286
00:13:40,277 --> 00:13:42,780
you take her back to her place,
you end up in the basement.

287
00:13:43,697 --> 00:13:44,990
With, like, the, the, the...

288
00:13:45,074 --> 00:13:47,451
the bleach, and the smell of Tide,

289
00:13:47,535 --> 00:13:49,161
-and mold.
-[Jerry laughs]

290
00:13:50,246 --> 00:13:53,999
You're to be, like, sexy and all... 
And all that.

291
00:13:54,375 --> 00:13:55,292
I think it's...

292
00:13:55,876 --> 00:13:58,754
one of the most powerful bits
that you have hit upon

293
00:13:59,338 --> 00:14:00,631
in your young career

294
00:14:01,674 --> 00:14:03,384
-is the company bit.
-Yeah.

295
00:14:03,717 --> 00:14:07,847
Twenty years ago, your doorbell rang,
that was a happy moment in your house.

296
00:14:07,930 --> 00:14:09,390
[audience laughter]

297
00:14:09,932 --> 00:14:11,433
It was called company.

298
00:14:11,517 --> 00:14:12,768
[audience laughter]

299
00:14:13,143 --> 00:14:16,272
That's the bit, I think, that really
kind of resonated with everybody online,

300
00:14:16,355 --> 00:14:18,482
-and that's kind of what really...
-[Jerry] Yeah.

301
00:14:18,566 --> 00:14:21,068
-...propelled a lot of--
-Isn't that amazing?

302
00:14:21,610 --> 00:14:23,779
It is a tremendously powerful bit.

303
00:14:24,613 --> 00:14:26,448
Your mother had a little Entemann's.

304
00:14:27,449 --> 00:14:29,577
There'd be some Sara Lee Crumble Cake.

305
00:14:29,994 --> 00:14:31,996
She made an announcement
when she bought it.

306
00:14:32,079 --> 00:14:34,290
She's like, "Listen,
nobody touch this cake.

307
00:14:34,373 --> 00:14:35,749
[audience laughter]

308
00:14:35,833 --> 00:14:37,293
This is for company only.

309
00:14:37,376 --> 00:14:39,503
Those crap muffins,
those are for you people."

310
00:14:39,587 --> 00:14:40,421
[audience laughter]

311
00:14:40,796 --> 00:14:42,256
The word doesn't exist anymore.

312
00:14:42,339 --> 00:14:43,340
-"Company?"
-Yeah.

313
00:14:44,592 --> 00:14:45,426
Doesn't exist.

314
00:14:46,051 --> 00:14:47,052
We're having company.

315
00:14:47,136 --> 00:14:48,178
-Yeah, it...
-Yeah.

316
00:14:48,262 --> 00:14:50,931
Yeah, you're right.
It's a word that kind of got lost.

317
00:14:51,599 --> 00:14:52,766
"That's for company."

318
00:14:52,975 --> 00:14:55,769
Everybody grew up with that,
hearing that in the house.

319
00:14:56,729 --> 00:14:57,980
You don't know it's gone.

320
00:14:58,063 --> 00:15:00,065
Nobody goes,
"We're having company tonight."

321
00:15:00,149 --> 00:15:01,108
No.

322
00:15:01,191 --> 00:15:03,027
Now your doorbell rings...

323
00:15:03,110 --> 00:15:04,945
[audience laughter]

324
00:15:05,988 --> 00:15:07,156
It's like, "What the [bleep]?"

325
00:15:07,239 --> 00:15:08,657
[audience laughter]

326
00:15:11,619 --> 00:15:12,661
[mouthing] Get down!

327
00:15:14,580 --> 00:15:16,290
[audience continues laughing]

328
00:15:22,087 --> 00:15:25,049
Get the sword in the living room. Somebody
get the sword underneath the couch

329
00:15:25,132 --> 00:15:26,634
in the living room. There's a sword.

330
00:15:28,969 --> 00:15:30,763
Oh, my God! What a day!

331
00:15:31,388 --> 00:15:33,432
This is nice!

332
00:15:40,481 --> 00:15:42,816
[Jerry] I'm so excited you're coming over
for dinner tonight.

333
00:15:42,900 --> 00:15:43,734
[Sebastian] Why?

334
00:15:43,943 --> 00:15:45,944
[Jerry] Because we're having company!

335
00:15:46,028 --> 00:15:47,196
[Sebastian laughs]

336
00:15:55,537 --> 00:15:57,831
[producer] Wait, hold on.
I got a battery warning.

337
00:15:58,958 --> 00:16:00,209
All right, see you later.

338
00:16:01,001 --> 00:16:02,336
Tammy, you're in my shot. 

